WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.300
Welcome back to Building
HR-Driven Identity.

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.738
This is Chapter 2: contractor
sources and enforced end dates.

3
00:00:07.738 --> 00:00:12.612
A contractor's end date in a spreadsheet
nobody updates keeps access alive.

4
00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:17.900
The end date the code computes, from every
fact that can end an engagement, doesn't.

5
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:21.465
So this chapter computes each
contractor's earliest end,

6
00:00:21.465 --> 00:00:26.622
writes the records as an extract the HR
middleware sends, reconciles the register,

7
00:00:26.622 --> 00:00:30.490
the card system and the directory
without changing anything,

8
00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:35.170
and builds a sponsor review that
removes access when no one responds.

9
00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.020
Why do contractors need their own code?

10
00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.645
Because they aren't in
the HR system's scope.

11
00:00:43.645 --> 00:00:46.223
They come from a register the agency owns,

12
00:00:46.223 --> 00:00:49.483
mapped into the same
canonical record as employees.

13
00:00:49.483 --> 00:00:54.214
The guide ships the contractor leaver
first: the middleware computes the

14
00:00:54.214 --> 00:00:58.558
earliest end and sends the leave
date, and an inactive status, itself.

15
00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:01.846
This chapter is that computation
and the checks around it.

16
00:01:01.846 --> 00:01:05.146
Which register to use is the
governance body's decision;

17
00:01:05.146 --> 00:01:08.020
the code works with
whichever one is named.

18
00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:12.670
Which cloud?

19
00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:17.443
The records reach Entra ID through the
contractor source's own provisioning app,

20
00:01:17.443 --> 00:01:20.008
through the same middleware employees use.

21
00:01:20.008 --> 00:01:25.797
Learn's feature availability page lists
API-driven inbound provisioning as available

22
00:01:25.797 --> 00:01:30.794
in Azure Government, and access reviews
too; GCC uses the commercial service.

23
00:01:30.794 --> 00:01:34.583
The bulk upload page itself
has no national cloud table.

24
00:01:34.583 --> 00:01:37.773
The middleware accepts only
the three Graph endpoints,

25
00:01:37.773 --> 00:01:41.171
and the reconciliation reads
users from the cloud you name.

26
00:01:41.171 --> 00:01:42.720
There's no default.

27
00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:48.445
Start with what Microsoft gives you.

28
00:01:48.445 --> 00:01:53.351
Learn's bulk upload page is the contract:
a SCIM post to the provisioning job,

29
00:01:53.351 --> 00:01:57.783
at most fifty user operations a
request and forty requests a second.

30
00:01:57.783 --> 00:02:02.347
The service matches on the attributes
the job maps, resolves the manager,

31
00:02:02.347 --> 00:02:05.770
and disables the user when
a record sends active false.

32
00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:10.970
A successful call returns accepted, with a
header pointing to the provisioning logs.

33
00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:15.845
For recertification, an assignment
policy's review settings carry reviewers,

34
00:02:15.845 --> 00:02:19.290
a schedule, and what happens
when nobody responds.

35
00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.690
Here's what Learn leaves to you.

36
00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:29.203
It disables an account when told to,
but not when an engagement has ended.

37
00:02:29.203 --> 00:02:35.178
It accepts any record, so refusing a
contractor with no end or no sponsor is yours.

38
00:02:35.178 --> 00:02:40.398
Mapping the register into the canonical
record, reconciling before anything changes,

39
00:02:40.398 --> 00:02:44.240
and writing the review settings
as a reviewed body are yours too.

40
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.115
Each row is pinned by a test.

41
00:02:46.115 --> 00:02:49.028
And the verdict: don't
write a second SCIM client.

42
00:02:49.028 --> 00:02:55.070
The existing middleware already validates,
batches, paces and records provenance.

43
00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:01.557
The heart of the chapter
is the earliest end.

44
00:03:01.557 --> 00:03:06.820
For each register row, the extract computes
every end condition that applies and

45
00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:10.904
takes the first: the contract end
or an earlier completion date,

46
00:03:10.904 --> 00:03:14.910
suitability withdrawn, a card
terminated, revoked or expired,

47
00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:19.858
a sponsor gone past the grace period, or
a sponsor recertification that's due.

48
00:03:19.858 --> 00:03:23.131
The thirty-day grace period
and the hundred-and-eighty-day

49
00:03:23.131 --> 00:03:25.519
cycle are design guidance the agency sets.

50
00:03:25.519 --> 00:03:29.638
A record past its end goes out
inactive, and every record carries

51
00:03:29.638 --> 00:03:33.800
its end as the termination date,
so the leaver fires that day.

52
00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:40.450
Two sets of rules sit
around that computation.

53
00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:44.217
The source checks quarantine
a record with no contract end,

54
00:03:44.217 --> 00:03:48.752
or whose sponsor isn't in the HR export,
warn when a sponsor has separated,

55
00:03:48.752 --> 00:03:51.750
and note an engagement
ending within two weeks.

56
00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:56.640
The reconciliation compares the register,
the card system and the directory:

57
00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.772
an enabled account past
its end, an orphan account,

58
00:03:59.772 --> 00:04:02.523
a card for someone the
register doesn't hold,

59
00:04:02.523 --> 00:04:06.725
a record with no account, and a leave
date that isn't the earliest end.

60
00:04:06.725 --> 00:04:09.650
It's read only, joined on the person key.

61
00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:17.154
The reconciliation makes one
Graph call: a filtered read of

62
00:04:17.154 --> 00:04:21.675
contractor users with their leave
dates, needing two read permissions.

63
00:04:21.675 --> 00:04:23.088
Then the sponsor review.

64
00:04:23.088 --> 00:04:27.760
The review command writes the review
settings for the contractor package's

65
00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:32.209
assignment policy: every six months,
the requestor's manager as reviewer,

66
00:04:32.209 --> 00:04:35.250
a named fallback, and remove
access on no response.

67
00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:40.375
So a missed recertification ends access,
just as the earliest-end rule says.

68
00:04:40.375 --> 00:04:41.850
The code sends nothing.

69
00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:44.079
Learn's cmdlet takes the whole policy,

70
00:04:44.079 --> 00:04:47.470
so you merge the block into
the policy you read back.

71
00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:55.683
The chapter's tests are seven, in the
writers-and-contractors test file.

72
00:04:55.683 --> 00:04:59.430
They check that the earliest
end takes the first condition,

73
00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:01.954
that an expired card ends an engagement,

74
00:05:01.954 --> 00:05:05.319
that a record with no end or
no sponsor is quarantined,

75
00:05:05.319 --> 00:05:07.766
that a duplicate person key is refused,

76
00:05:07.766 --> 00:05:12.737
that the extract matches the middleware's
layout and marks past ends inactive,

77
00:05:12.737 --> 00:05:15.490
that the reconciliation finds every class,

78
00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:19.008
and that the review removes
access when no one responds.

79
00:05:19.008 --> 00:05:24.370
All fifty-seven tests in the kit pass
with no package, tenant or network.

80
00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:31.695
The verified facts, each
read on November first.

81
00:05:31.695 --> 00:05:36.601
Bulk upload is a version one point
oh API: fifty operations a request,

82
00:05:36.601 --> 00:05:41.683
forty requests a second, and an
accepted response pointing to the logs.

83
00:05:41.683 --> 00:05:44.920
Its least permission is the
owned-by upload permission.

84
00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:49.431
The review settings and the access
review procedure are both documented,

85
00:05:49.431 --> 00:05:52.758
and access reviews are
available in Azure Government.

86
00:05:52.758 --> 00:05:57.070
The requestor manager subject set
is documented for approval stages.

87
00:05:57.070 --> 00:06:02.160
And the policy update cmdlet needs the
entitlement management read-write permission.

88
00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:08.798
Two places Learn's pages
differ or say nothing.

89
00:06:08.798 --> 00:06:11.586
First, the requestor's
manager as a reviewer:

90
00:06:11.586 --> 00:06:15.612
Learn documents that subject set
for approvals, and the portal

91
00:06:15.612 --> 00:06:19.948
offers Manager as a reviewer, but no
page shows it in review settings.

92
00:06:19.948 --> 00:06:21.486
Lab 2 confirms it.

93
00:06:21.486 --> 00:06:25.826
Second, bulk upload in the national
clouds: the API page has no table,

94
00:06:25.826 --> 00:06:31.272
and the program reads the feature availability
page as saying it's available there.

95
00:06:31.272 --> 00:06:33.673
And remember the code is illustrative:

96
00:06:33.673 --> 00:06:38.750
it runs on fictional data and doesn't
prove a tenant maps the same attributes.

97
00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:43.950
What does the output prove?

98
00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:49.085
For account management and third-party
personnel security, AC-2 and PS-7,

99
00:06:49.085 --> 00:06:54.810
the code gives every earliest end with its
reason, and a reconciliation run before

100
00:06:54.810 --> 00:07:00.450
the contractor leaver ships and again after,
with no enabled account past its end.

101
00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:03.213
Keep both output folders
with their manifests.

102
00:07:03.213 --> 00:07:07.237
The worker type on every record
supports identifier management.

103
00:07:07.237 --> 00:07:11.449
The register as one named source
per fact supports the OMB memos,

104
00:07:11.449 --> 00:07:14.388
though the signed
authority table isn't code.

105
00:07:14.388 --> 00:07:18.410
And the privacy office's
determination covers the data flows.

106
00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:23.085
Who does what?

107
00:07:23.085 --> 00:07:27.147
Developers build the contractor
source's integration,

108
00:07:27.147 --> 00:07:29.885
and need to read Python, CSV and JSON.

109
00:07:29.885 --> 00:07:33.535
The owner of the contractor
register signs the authority table,

110
00:07:33.535 --> 00:07:36.910
and the governance body names
which register is the source.

111
00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:40.802
Identity engineers run the
provisioning app, and the middleware's

112
00:07:40.802 --> 00:07:44.623
identity holds the upload permission
on the contractor app only.

113
00:07:44.623 --> 00:07:49.337
The I S S O keeps the reconciliation
folders and the provenance manifest,

114
00:07:49.337 --> 00:07:55.120
the privacy office covers the data flows,
and every quarantined record has an owner.

115
00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:00.770
Lab 1 runs on a laptop.

116
00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:05.283
Step one lists the reconciliation's
one read and its two permissions.

117
00:08:05.283 --> 00:08:09.504
Step two computes the earliest
ends: nine records, three active,

118
00:08:09.504 --> 00:08:13.481
four inactive past their earliest
end, and two quarantined,

119
00:08:13.481 --> 00:08:16.971
with an exit code of two
because the run has findings.

120
00:08:16.971 --> 00:08:19.245
Step three reads the decisions.

121
00:08:19.245 --> 00:08:24.645
One record has no contract end;
another's sponsor isn't in the HR export.

122
00:08:24.645 --> 00:08:29.007
And one shows the rule at work: its
card expired on October twenty-eighth,

123
00:08:29.007 --> 00:08:33.660
but its recertification was due three
days earlier, which comes first.

124
00:08:37.410 --> 00:08:40.260
Step four hands the
extract to the middleware,

125
00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:44.085
which builds seven records in
one batch, four of them disables.

126
00:08:44.085 --> 00:08:47.885
Nothing is sent: the middleware's
dry run is its default.

127
00:08:47.885 --> 00:08:50.381
Step five reconciles the register,

128
00:08:50.381 --> 00:08:54.598
the cards and the directory:
seven findings and one warning.

129
00:08:54.598 --> 00:08:57.861
Four accounts are still enabled
past their earliest end.

130
00:08:57.861 --> 00:09:02.156
There's an orphan account, a card for
someone the source doesn't hold, a leave

131
00:09:02.156 --> 00:09:06.522
date that would fire the leaver on the
wrong day, and a record with no account.

132
00:09:06.522 --> 00:09:10.010
Those four accounts are
exactly the four disables.

133
00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:18.135
Step six writes the sponsor review
settings for review, and sends nothing.

134
00:09:18.135 --> 00:09:22.890
The block removes access on no
response, names the requestor's manager

135
00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:27.235
at level one as the primary reviewer,
and recurs every six months.

136
00:09:27.235 --> 00:09:31.235
Step seven runs the whole
kit's tests: fifty-seven pass.

137
00:09:31.235 --> 00:09:33.430
Then delete the output folder.

138
00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:40.792
Lab 2 uses a test tenant;
the authors haven't run it.

139
00:09:40.792 --> 00:09:43.117
Set up the contractor provisioning app,

140
00:09:43.117 --> 00:09:47.343
and grant the middleware the owned-by
upload permission on that app only.

141
00:09:47.343 --> 00:09:51.335
Send two test contractors, one
active and one past its end,

142
00:09:51.335 --> 00:09:53.543
and read the provisioning logs.

143
00:09:53.543 --> 00:09:55.168
Reconcile against the tenant:

144
00:09:55.168 --> 00:09:58.418
there should be no finding for
the account you just disabled.

145
00:09:58.418 --> 00:10:02.782
Then apply the review settings by merging
them into the policy you read back,

146
00:10:02.782 --> 00:10:05.068
and confirm the reviewer is the manager.

147
00:10:05.068 --> 00:10:08.320
Finally, restore the policy and clean up.

148
00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:16.145
The dangers all come down to access
outliving the reason for it.

149
00:10:16.145 --> 00:10:20.748
A trusted spreadsheet end date lets
access outlive the engagement;

150
00:10:20.748 --> 00:10:23.132
the computed earliest end stops it.

151
00:10:23.132 --> 00:10:26.745
An open-ended contractor never
leaves, so it's quarantined.

152
00:10:26.745 --> 00:10:31.920
A sponsor who left leaves nobody accountable,
so access ends after the grace period.

153
00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:34.732
A missed recertification removes access.

154
00:10:34.732 --> 00:10:39.469
A drifted leave date is caught, and so
is a card for someone nobody sponsors.

155
00:10:39.469 --> 00:10:41.823
And a second SCIM client
means two writers,

156
00:10:41.823 --> 00:10:44.820
so everything goes through
the one middleware.

157
00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:49.920
Here's what done looks like.

158
00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:54.245
The contractor source is named and
its owner signed the authority table.

159
00:10:54.245 --> 00:10:58.933
The rules file holds the agency's
grace period and recertification cycle.

160
00:10:58.933 --> 00:11:01.436
The extract runs on the
middleware's schedule,

161
00:11:01.436 --> 00:11:03.544
and quarantined records have an owner.

162
00:11:03.544 --> 00:11:08.149
The reconciliation ran before and
after the contractor leaver shipped,

163
00:11:08.149 --> 00:11:10.758
with no account left enabled past its end.

164
00:11:10.758 --> 00:11:14.799
The review settings are confirmed
by read-back, the upload permission

165
00:11:14.799 --> 00:11:18.770
is scoped to the contractor app, and
the privacy office has signed off.

166
00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:22.257
Next, Chapter 3: joiner workflows as code.

167
00:11:22.257 --> 00:11:23.420
Thanks for watching.
